From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Bates <sbates@raithlin.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] net: devmem: detach TX bindings on NETDEV_UNREGISTER

Unregistering a net_device while a devmem TX binding is live leaves
binding->dev (and binding->vdev) pointing at freed memory. When the
process that created the binding then closes its netlink socket,
netdev_nl_sock_priv_destroy() reads binding->dev and calls netdev_hold()
on it:

  BUG: KASAN: use-after-free in netdev_nl_sock_priv_destroy+0x1b1/0x1e0
  Read of size 8 at addr ffff8881362386c0 by task devmem_tx/69808
  Call Trace:
   kasan_report+0xe4/0x1a0
   netdev_nl_sock_priv_destroy+0x1b1/0x1e0
   genl_release+0xf5/0x190
   netlink_release+0xcd1/0x18e0
   __sock_release+0xb0/0x270
   sock_close+0x18/0x20
   __fput+0x365/0xa90
   __x64_sys_close+0x7d/0xd0

  BUG: unable to handle page fault for address: ffff88829fd34000
  Oops: Oops: 0002 [#1] SMP KASAN NOPTI
  RIP: 0010:netdev_nl_sock_priv_destroy+0xbd/0x1e0

Root cause: an RX binding has its binding->dev cleared from
mp_dmabuf_devmem_uninstall(), which runs when the last RX queue the
binding is installed on is torn down. A TX binding (NETDEV_CMD_BIND_TX)
has no bound RX queue, so that path never runs for it and nothing in
netdev unregistration detaches it. The binding outlives the device: it
stays on the netlink socket's binding list, and TCP retransmits of
already-queued devmem skbs can keep it alive well past the unregister.
Everything that later looks at binding->dev - the socket-close teardown
above, and the dst_dev comparison in net_devmem_get_binding() - is
looking at freed memory.

How it was found: testing devmem TCP TX against the rocm-ernic userspace
emulated NIC (libvfio-user, ionic device model), on two guests with a
KASAN kernel. "rmmod ionic" four seconds into a devmem TX stream
unloads cleanly; the oops comes when the sender process exits and closes
its netlink socket. It reproduces on every attempt, and it also fires
when the sender is killed with SIGKILL rather than exiting normally.
This is in the networking core, not in the ionic driver: ionic is no
longer loaded when the oops happens, and no driver patch is involved.

The same bug is in mainline and in v7.2.15, the ERNIC bundle kernel.

Backport of net-next commit ede1618064a9 ("net: devmem: detach TX
bindings on NETDEV_UNREGISTER") by Mina Almasry <almasrymina@google.com>,
merged via Paolo Abeni. Note that two commits named by an earlier study
do NOT fix this: commit 23fe6ac2e97a ("net: skbuff: make
skb_copy_and_csum_bits() report unreadable frags") and commit
7fec569df355 ("net: skbuff: fail segmentation when a segment can't be
checksummed") are skb_segment()/checksum fixes for the SG-off and
checksum-off paths, and touch neither net/core/devmem.c nor
net/core/netdev-genl.c.

The upstream patch does not apply to v7.2.15; this is a hand-backport.
Differences, all forced by how much net/core/devmem.c has changed since
v7.2.15:

 - Upstream extends an existing net_devmem_dev_unregister() that handles
   RX bindings. v7.2.15 has no such function and no NETDEV_UNREGISTER
   hook in net/core/netdev-genl.c at all, so the function is added here
   along with its call site, its declaration and its !CONFIG_NET_DEVMEM
   stub. The RX half of upstream's loop is omitted: at v7.2.15 RX
   bindings are already detached by mp_dmabuf_devmem_uninstall(), which
   dev_memory_provider_uninstall() runs just before the NETDEV_UNREGISTER
   notifier.
 - Upstream clears the binding's NET_DEVMEM_BINDING_LIVE xarray mark and
   uses __xa_clear_mark() under the xarray lock. v7.2.15 has no such
   mark (net_devmem_unbind_dmabuf() erases the binding from the xarray
   instead), so there is nothing to clear.
 - Upstream also unmaps the dma_buf attachment here, via its
   net_devmem_dmabuf_binding_unmap() helper. v7.2.15 has no such helper:
   the unmap is open-coded in __net_devmem_dmabuf_binding_free() with no
   guard that would make a second unmap safe. Adding an early unmap
   would mean backporting that helper and its idempotency, so this
   backport deliberately does only the pointer detach, which is the
   memory-safety half. The consequence is that the dma_buf stays mapped
   until the last devmem skb drops its reference to the binding, so the
   DMA-API debug warning "device driver has pending DMA allocations
   while released from device" that an unload with a live binding
   already produces on v7.2.15 remains. That is a debug warning, not a
   use-after-free.
 - v7.2.15 writes binding->dev under binding->lock (a mutex) and
   netdev_nl_sock_priv_destroy() reads it under the same mutex before
   netdev_hold(), so the clearing here takes binding->lock too. That
   cannot be done under the xarray spinlock, so the loop drops the
   xarray lock around the sleeping section and holds a percpu_ref on the
   binding across it, which upstream does not need. Lock order is
   netdev_lock(dev) -> binding->lock; netdev_nl_sock_priv_destroy()
   releases binding->lock before taking netdev_lock(), so there is no
   inversion.

Upstream was reproduced and verified with a netdevsim selftest; this
backport was compile-tested only (see the README beside this patch for
what was and was not run).

Fixes: bd61848900bf ("net: devmem: Implement TX path")
Fixes: 1abe839b34ae ("net: devmem: support TX over NETMEM_TX_NO_DMA devices")
---
 net/core/devmem.c      | 59 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 net/core/devmem.h      |  5 ++++
 net/core/netdev-genl.c |  1 +
 3 files changed, 65 insertions(+)

diff --git a/net/core/devmem.c b/net/core/devmem.c
index 957d6b9..72a6af0 100644
--- a/net/core/devmem.c
+++ b/net/core/devmem.c
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 #include <linux/mm.h>
 #include <linux/netdevice.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
+#include <net/netdev_lock.h>
 #include <net/netdev_queues.h>
 #include <net/netdev_rx_queue.h>
 #include <net/page_pool/helpers.h>
@@ -151,6 +152,64 @@ void net_devmem_unbind_dmabuf(struct net_devmem_dmabuf_binding *binding)
 	percpu_ref_kill(&binding->ref);
 }
 
+void net_devmem_dev_unregister(struct net_device *dev)
+{
+	struct net_devmem_dmabuf_binding *binding;
+	unsigned long xa_idx;
+
+	netdev_assert_locked(dev);
+
+	if (READ_ONCE(dev->reg_state) != NETREG_UNREGISTERING)
+		return;
+
+	/* An RX binding drops its reference to the device from
+	 * mp_dmabuf_devmem_uninstall(), when the last queue it is installed
+	 * on is torn down. A TX binding has no bound queue, so nothing
+	 * clears binding->dev or binding->vdev for it and both dangle once
+	 * the net_device is freed.
+	 */
+	xa_lock(&net_devmem_dmabuf_bindings);
+	xa_for_each(&net_devmem_dmabuf_bindings, xa_idx, binding) {
+		if (binding->direction != DMA_TO_DEVICE ||
+		    (READ_ONCE(binding->dev) != dev &&
+		     READ_ONCE(binding->vdev) != dev))
+			continue;
+
+		/* The binding may be unbound and freed once the xarray lock
+		 * is dropped; hold it over the sleeping section.
+		 */
+		if (!net_devmem_dmabuf_binding_get(binding))
+			continue;
+
+		xa_unlock(&net_devmem_dmabuf_bindings);
+
+		/* binding->lock keeps this atomic against the netdev_hold()
+		 * in netdev_nl_sock_priv_destroy(), which either takes a
+		 * reference before the device is gone or sees NULL.
+		 */
+		mutex_lock(&binding->lock);
+		if (READ_ONCE(binding->dev) == dev) {
+			/* The physical device is going away: no TX can
+			 * reach this binding through either name.
+			 */
+			WRITE_ONCE(binding->vdev, NULL);
+			WRITE_ONCE(binding->dev, NULL);
+		} else if (READ_ONCE(binding->vdev) == dev) {
+			/* Only the virtual device is going away: stop new
+			 * sends matching it and let the in-flight ones on
+			 * binding->dev finish.
+			 */
+			WRITE_ONCE(binding->vdev, NULL);
+		}
+		mutex_unlock(&binding->lock);
+
+		net_devmem_dmabuf_binding_put(binding);
+
+		xa_lock(&net_devmem_dmabuf_bindings);
+	}
+	xa_unlock(&net_devmem_dmabuf_bindings);
+}
+
 int net_devmem_bind_dmabuf_to_queue(struct net_device *dev, u32 rxq_idx,
 				    struct net_devmem_dmabuf_binding *binding,
 				    struct netlink_ext_ack *extack)
diff --git a/net/core/devmem.h b/net/core/devmem.h
index 3852a56..12c922e 100644
--- a/net/core/devmem.h
+++ b/net/core/devmem.h
@@ -97,6 +97,7 @@ net_devmem_bind_dmabuf(struct net_device *dev, void *vdev,
 		       struct netlink_ext_ack *extack);
 struct net_devmem_dmabuf_binding *net_devmem_lookup_dmabuf(u32 id);
 void net_devmem_unbind_dmabuf(struct net_devmem_dmabuf_binding *binding);
+void net_devmem_dev_unregister(struct net_device *dev);
 int net_devmem_bind_dmabuf_to_queue(struct net_device *dev, u32 rxq_idx,
 				    struct net_devmem_dmabuf_binding *binding,
 				    struct netlink_ext_ack *extack);
@@ -191,6 +192,10 @@ net_devmem_unbind_dmabuf(struct net_devmem_dmabuf_binding *binding)
 {
 }
 
+static inline void net_devmem_dev_unregister(struct net_device *dev)
+{
+}
+
 static inline int
 net_devmem_bind_dmabuf_to_queue(struct net_device *dev, u32 rxq_idx,
 				struct net_devmem_dmabuf_binding *binding,
diff --git a/net/core/netdev-genl.c b/net/core/netdev-genl.c
index c15d8d4..3f9ec97 100644
--- a/net/core/netdev-genl.c
+++ b/net/core/netdev-genl.c
@@ -1462,6 +1462,7 @@ static int netdev_genl_netdevice_event(struct notifier_block *nb,
 		break;
 	case NETDEV_UNREGISTER:
 		netdev_lock(netdev);
+		net_devmem_dev_unregister(netdev);
 		netdev_genl_dev_notify(netdev, NETDEV_CMD_DEV_DEL_NTF);
 		netdev_unlock(netdev);
 		break;
-- 
2.43.0

